28. Partitioning

Spark divides data into partitions.

Dataset
   ↓
-------------------------
| Part 1 | Part 2 | Part 3 |
-------------------------

Each partition can be processed independently.

Check:

rdd.getNumPartitions()

Change partitions:

rdd.repartition(4)

or:

rdd.coalesce(2)
29. repartition() vs coalesce()
repartition
df.repartition(10)

Can increase/decrease partitions.

Usually involves a shuffle.

coalesce
df.coalesce(2)

Usually used to reduce partitions with less movement of data.

Remember:

repartition → expensive shuffle
coalesce    → mainly reduce partitions
30. Shuffle

One of the most important performance concepts.

Suppose:

df.groupBy("department").count()

Data may need to move between executors so that all rows belonging to the same department are brought together.

Executor 1 ──────┐
Executor 2 ──────┼──→ Shuffle → Group
Executor 3 ──────┘

This data movement is called:

Shuffle

Shuffle is expensive because of:

Network I/O
Disk I/O
Serialization
Memory usage
31. Narrow vs Wide Transformations
Narrow transformation

Data doesn't need significant movement between partitions.

Examples:

map()
filter()

Conceptually:

Partition 1 → Partition 1
Partition 2 → Partition 2
Wide transformation

Data needs to move between partitions.

Examples:

groupByKey()
reduceByKey()
join()
distinct()

Conceptually:

Partition 1 ──┐
Partition 2 ──┼──→ Shuffle
Partition 3 ──┘
Remember
Narrow → little/no shuffle
Wide   → shuffle
32. reduceByKey() vs groupByKey()

Suppose:

data = [
    ("A", 10),
    ("A", 20),
    ("B", 30)
]
groupByKey
rdd.groupByKey()

conceptually:

A → [10, 20]
B → [30]
reduceByKey
rdd.reduceByKey(lambda a, b: a + b)

produces:

A → 30
B → 30
Important

reduceByKey() can perform local aggregation before shuffle.

reduceByKey
     ↓
local combine
     ↓
less data shuffled
     ↓
better performance

So generally:

reduceByKey > groupByKey

for aggregation tasks.

