39. Spark Performance Checklist

When Spark is slow:

1. Check partitions
2. Check shuffle
3. Avoid unnecessary collect()
4. Filter early
5. Select only required columns
6. Use Parquet
7. Avoid groupByKey when possible
8. Cache reused data
9. Check joins
10. Use appropriate partitioning
40. Filter Early

Bad:

df.select("*") \
  .join(huge_df, "id") \
  .filter(df.country == "India")

Better conceptually:

df.filter(df.country == "India") \
  .join(huge_df, "id")

Why?

Filter first
 ↓
less data
 ↓
less shuffle
 ↓
less processing
 ↓
faster
41. Select Only Required Columns

Instead of:

df.select("*")

use:

df.select(
    "customer_id",
    "name",
    "salary"
)

Less data:

Storage
 ↓
Network
 ↓
Memory
 ↓
Processing
42. Broadcast Join

If one table is small:

from pyspark.sql.functions import broadcast

df_large.join(
    broadcast(df_small),
    "id"
)

Instead of shuffling the small table around, Spark can distribute it to executors.

Conceptually:

Small table
    ↓
Broadcast
 ↓   ↓   ↓
E1  E2  E3

Large data stays distributed

Useful when one side of a join is genuinely small enough to broadcast.

